charitable_objects:
The principal object for which the Company is established is the advancement of health, education and religion to be achieved primarily by providing:
(a)	vocational courses in sacramental marriage preparation according to the Catholic Churchs teaching on marriage;
(b)	marriage and relationship and ancillary counselling services;
(c)	relationship education programmes in schools and elsewhere from the perspective of the Catholic Christian tradition; and
(d)	any other services whose purpose is to promote and support marriage and the family in accordance with the Churchs vision of marriage as a vocation of life and love.

The main objects for which the Company are established are the advancement of health, education and religion in the Catholic Archdiocese of Dublin to be achieved primarily by:
(1)	taking over the mission of the unincorporated association known as ACCORD Catholic Marriage Care Service insofar as it relates to the delivery of services by that association in the Catholic Archdiocese of Dublin;
(2)	providing marriage preparation courses according to the teaching of the Catholic Church on marriage to couples preparing for the Sacrament of Marriage;
(3)	providing marriage enrichment courses for couples seeking a deeper commitment within the Sacrament of Marriage;
(4)	providing relationship education programmes in schools, parishes and elsewhere from the perspective of the Catholic Christian tradition; 
(5)	providing marriage, family, relationship and ancillary counselling services to enable individuals, couples and families to identify and acknowledge difficulties in their relationship and to assist them in resolving those difficulties;
(6)	serving the pastoral needs of individuals, couples and families at various stages in their marriage and relationships through facilitating and empowering them to explore and reflect on:
(a)	the purpose and value of their choices within or towards a commitment to marriage in the Catholic tradition; 
(b)	responsible parenthood within a faith context; 
(c)	enhancing each family members emotional, spiritual and psychological well-being; and 
(d)	developing good communication and conflict resolution skills;
(7)	working with any agency of the Catholic Church caring for couples facing the challenges of Christian marriage and helping to sustain and enrich positive family values and Christian living and
(8)	providing any other services whose purpose is to promote and support marriage and the family in accordance in accordance with the Church's vision of manage as a vision of life and love.

2.	The main object for which the Body is established is the advancement of religion in Ireland, in particular by:
(1)	welcoming people to celebrate Mass (reference to the Mass or the Eucharist refers to the Roman Catholic liturgy) with a particular welcome for LGBT (lesbian, gay, bisexual, and transgendered) people, their parents, family members, and friends;
(2)	publicising the fact that Masses with that particular welcome are being celebrated;
(3)	helping people to feel that they belong in a praying community and to experience full participation when celebrating the Eucharist; 
(4)	helping people to deepen their spiritual and personal lives and relationships with God, their parents, family members, and friends, and with others in their wider communities; and
(5)	providing assistance when thought appropriate to any trust, company or body of persons established for purposes that are regarded as exclusively charitable under Irish law, including those charities for whom collections are generally taken up at Masses in Ireland.

MAIN OBJECT
The main object for which the Charity is established is the advancement of religion - by worship, prayer and our way of life to witness to the transcendence of God and to advance the religious and charitable work carried on by the Charity in Ireland and elsewhere in accordance with the spirituality and values of the Discalced Nuns of the Order of the Blessed Virgin Mary of Mount Carmel.  
SUBSIDIARY OBJECTS:
To promote the spiritual, moral and intellectual wellbeing of the members;	
To promote transcendent values which give meaning and hope to the people of our time;	
To assist in the relief of poverty and its consequences in areas of natural disasters;	
To provide comfort and support to the poor and distressed through prayer, personal contact, listening and correspondence;
To support through contemplative prayer: those who spread the Gospel, the good of the Church and the good of the whole human family;
To offer prayer for the deceased members, relatives, friends and benefactors of the Order, in accordance with the spirituality and values of the Order;
To witness to and nurture the Christian Faith in both the local and wider community through the provision of worship and prayer space in the Chapel of the Monastery and to facilitate their participation in liturgies;
To produce altar breads and other Church articles for sale and as a service to churches and parishes;
To promote the protection of the environment by a simple lifestyle and by encouraging awareness on the necessity of protecting the environment
To promote vocations to the Order and to educate and train both current and new Members of the Charity;
To provide for the reasonable needs and assistance of Members of the Charity who serve the main objects of the charity and to provide them with the necessary resources required for the performance of their duties in keeping with the main object of the Charity, PROVIDED ALWAYS that no part of the capital or income of the Charity may be distributed to any person by way of profit or dividend;
To provide services and nursing care for the sick and elderly members of the Charity in accordance with the core values of the Charity;
To provide assistance to other bodies involved in charitable works having a main object similar to those of the Charity; and
Generally to respond to the needs of people within the mission of the Christian Church throughout the world PROVIDED ALWAYS that at all times the Charity shall carry out the aforesaid purposes and objects in a manner exclusively charitable within the law of the Republic of Ireland.

The main objects of the Trust are to advance the Christian faith in Ireland according to the spirit and ideals of Edmund Rice and to that end the following subsidiary objects:
a) To provide or assist in the provision of education of any child or person in keeping with the precepts of the Christian Faith;
b) To provide assistance as the Trustees may from time to time think fit for the provision of educational facilities;
c) To provide assistance to children or other persons especially those who are poor by the provision of accommodation, training, instruction, or pastoral care in order to develop their physical, mental and spiritual capacities;
d) To provide assistance for training of teachers, parents, youth leaders or other persons engaged or to be engaged in the provision of education, leadership, development, or instruction to children and other persons;
e) To provide assistance required for furtherance or development of education or educational research into educational disadvantage, school effectiveness, youth leadership and religious development of young people;
f) To provide for the reasonable needs and assistance of those who have served the main objects of the Charity and to provide them with the necessary resources required in the performance of their duties in keeping with the main objects of the Charity provided always that no part of the capital or income of the Trust Fund may be distributed to any person by way of profit or dividend;
g) To advance the Christian faith in any part of the world;
h) To assist in the relief of poverty in Ireland or any part of the world;
i) To provide assistance to other bodies involved in charitable works having main objects similar to the Trust;
j) To provide assistance for any other purpose which the Trustees deem to be beneficial to the community having regard to the main objects of the Trust.
Provided always that all times the Trustees shall carry out the aforesaid purposes and objects in a manner exclusively charitable within the law of the Republic of Ireland.

Coláiste an Spioraid Naoimh is a Catholic school operating under the trusteeship of the Presentation Brothers Schools Trust (P.B.S.T.) which seeks to create a caring environment in which Christian values permeate all aspects of school life and teachers and pupils find fulfilment.  The school believes in the vital importance of good relationships between teachers, students and parents and is conscious of its role in the broader community.  The school recognizes the need for the on-going professional development of its staff to meet the challenges of a changing world.
The school endeavours to:
 provide students with opportunities to live their faith through liturgy, prayer and reflection;
 inculcate good standards of behaviour and respect for one another;
 promote academic excellence to the full potential of the individual  student;
 foster the growth of self-worth and confidence, through the development of the whole person; 
 provide access to the resources of civilization;
 equip students with life-skills and develop their leadership qualities; 
 provide an opportunity for the physical development of each student.
The school hopes to achieve the above through a curriculum which provides well-structured learning experiences, both academic and non-academic.

(a) To advance religion through the maintenance of Friary, Priests, Brothers and related parties;
(b) The relief of poverty, in particular among the destitute; and,
(c) The advancement of the Roman Catholic Faith in Ireland and elsewhere.

The main object for which the company is established is to advance the religious, social,  educational and other charitable works carried on by the Congregation of Our Lady of the Missions (the Congregation) in accordance with the charism, spirit and ethos of its Foundress Euphrasie Barbier, in Ireland and throughout the world and to provide for Members throughout their lives within the Congregation.
The subsidiary objects for which the Company are established are, so far and so far only as the same are legally charitable and in furtherance of the main object:
To promote the spiritual, moral, and intellectual wellbeing of the Members of the Congregation;
To assist in the relief of poverty and its consequences in areas of the mission of the Congregation and in areas of natural disasters; 
To provide funding to overseas missions in accordance with the core values and ethos of the Congregation;
To provide pastoral care to schools, local communities, the sick and elderly, parishes and youth ministries to which the Members of the Province belong in accordance with the core values and ethos of the Congregation;
To assist with the provision and advancement of Catholic education provided by the Notre Dame Schools Trust;
To provide support and assistance in the safeguarding of children and vulnerable adults and ensuring children and vulnerable adults are well cared for while in the care of any ministry of the Congregation;
To witness and encourage the religious commitment of people to the Christian faith in both the local and wider community by promoting prayer and local community social activities;
To assist in the protection of the natural environment by promoting and encouraging public awareness of the importance of protecting the environment in all ministries and activities carried out by the Congregation both in Ireland and throughout the world;
To assist in the formation and training of members of the voluntary association of women known as the Congregation of Our Lady of the Missions to be engaged in the provision and delivery of educational and welfare services particularly to the poor and marginalised;
To provide services and nursing care for sick and elderly Members of the Congregation in accordance with the core values and ethos of the Congregation of Our Lady of the Missions;
To provide for the reasonable needs and assistance of Members and former Members of the Congregation who serve, or have served, the main objects of the Company and to provide them with the necessary resources required in the performance of their duties in keeping with the main object of the Company PROVIDED ALWAYS that no part of the capital or income of the Company may be distributed to any person by way of profit or dividend; 
To provide assistance to other bodies involved in charitable works having a main object similar to those of the Company; and
Generally to respond to the needs of people within the mission of the Roman Catholic Church throughout the world PROVIDED ALWAYS that at all times the company shall carry out the aforesaid purposes and objects in a manner exclusively charitable within the law of the Republic of Ireland.

3.1.	The Charitable Objects of the Diocesan Trust are the advancement of the Roman Catholic religion in the Diocese and the advancement of charitable purposes congruent with the advancement of religion and which are supported by the Church throughout the world or in any part of the world.

3.2.	In furtherance exclusively of the foregoing Charitable Objects, the Diocesan Trust shall have the following subsidiary objects:

a)	to promote the advancement of religion by enabling the Bishop to fulfil his Canon Law responsibilities in relation to the advancement or maintenance of the Roman Catholic religion in any way which he considers appropriate including but not limited to:

1.	advancement of religion;

2.	advancement of education;

3.	provision of Priests and other clergy; 

4.	promotion of Catholic ethos in all walks of life;

5.	relief of poverty; and

6.	provision of physical infrastructure including but not limited to churches, parish centres, schools, hospitals, hostels for the homeless, places of accommodation, cemeteries and office premises whether directly or through any other charitable body or company and to encourage, promote, set up and support activities, buildings, institutions and services of a pastoral kind and nature in the Diocese including but not limited to the following services: 

a.	the development and renewal of any parish or parishes; 
b.	marriage counselling and tribunal services;
c.	vocations for the priesthood and religious life (for example by providing financial assistance for seminaries, seminarians, deacons, religious institutes and religious); 
d.	faith development;
e.	communications;
f.	services to the travelling people;
g.	assistance to emigrants and immigrants;
h.	religious education;
i.	diocesan committees, commissions and councils; 
j.	chaplaincies; 
k.	the provision of safeguarding services and training; 
l.	the provision of training of pastoral workers and volunteers; 
m.	supporting the work of the Irish Catholic Bishops’ Conference (also known as the Irish Episcopal Conference); and
n.	supporting the work of Trócaire.
in each case in accordance with Canon Law.

The main object for which the company is established is to advance the Christian faith in Ireland according to the spirit and ideals of the Blessed Edmund Rice, and in the spirit of the Congregation of the Christian Brothers and the Congregation of the Presentation Brothers.
The following objects set out hereafter are exclusively subsidiary and ancillary to the main object set out above and these objects are to be used only for the attainment of that main object and any income generated therefrom is to be applied for the main object only:
a)	To provide or assist in the provision of education of any child or other person, and to assist in the furtherance or development of education or educational research;
b)	To assist in the provision and delivery of educational and welfare services, particularly for the poor, marginalised, the youth and the aged;
c)	To provide assistance to other bodies involved in charitable works having main objects similar to the company;
d)	To provide or participate in such educational or welfare activities as are considered to be of assistance and benefit to the community in furthering the principal main objects of the company;
e)	To take all lawful steps for raising funds for the purpose of the main objects of the company and to ensure against any risks associated with any fundraising events;
f)	To advance the Christian faith in Ireland by restoring, developing, maintaining, and operating heritage centres, and to identify, collect, and record historical information on Blessed Edmund Rice and provide such information to the public;
g)	To stimulate and inspire members of the Congregation of Christian Brothers and Congregation of Presentation Brothers, their associates and colleagues, in ministry to continue the work of Edmund Rice in ways that are appropriate to the world for the time being;
h)	To operate the heritage centres, which shall comprise heritage, renewal, hospitality, pilgrimage, archives, and ministries; and
i)	To increase the members of the Congregation of Christian Brothers and Presentation Brothers awareness and experience of their global interdependence and to promote solidarity amongst members of the two Congregations.

The main object for which the Body is established (the “Main Object”) is for the public benefit through the advancement of education and the prevention or relief of poverty or economic hardship in Palestine, where the provision of university graduates is vital for its economic development and for the general benefit of its communities.  FBUI supports the education and learning of individuals irrespective of their religion, gender, ethnicity, age or sexual orientation.  In particular, FBUI supports the mission of Bethlehem University by being the country-based node for Ireland of volunteers and the local advisory/endowment board of the global Bethlehem University International Support Network.
Bethlehem University), a Catholic Christian co-educational institution of higher learning founded in 1973 in the Lasallian tradition, is open to students of all faith traditions.  Bethlehem University aspires to serve its students in their integral development and to contribute to building a free, peaceful and vibrant Palestine.  The Bethlehem University International Support Network (“BU-ISN”) serves as a strategic, coordinated initiative to promote global collaboration, enhance visibility, and secure philanthropic support for BU. FBUI is the country-based node for Ireland of BU-ISN.  The purpose and mission of BU-ISN is to:
(a)	enhance global awareness and support for the mission and academic excellence of Bethlehem University;
(b)	coordinate strategic fundraising and engagement activities across its country-based nodes, such as FBUI in Ireland;
(c)	strengthen relationships with alumni, benefactors, and institutional partners;
(d)	promote a culture of transparency, accountability, and global collaboration in support of the University.

The principal object for which the Company is established is to apply such funds as may be  donated by the Institute of the Brothers of the Christian Schools and others for the following charitable purposes:-
(a)	The benefit of the charitable works of the International Institute of the Brothers of the Christian Schools;
(b)	the support of charitable programs that are sponsored and certified by the International Institute of the Brothers of the Christian Schools;
(c)	the assistance and general aid of the organisation and administrative needs, that are the good function of any part of the Institute of the Brothers of the Christian Schools. 	
The principal object for which the Company is established is to apply such funds as may be  donated by the Institute of the Brothers of the Christian Schools and others for the following charitable purposes:-
(a)	The benefit of the charitable works of the International Institute of the Brothers of the Christian Schools;
(b)	the support of charitable programs that are sponsored and certified by the International Institute of the Brothers of the Christian Schools;
(c)	the assistance and general aid of the organisation and administrative needs, that are the good function of any part of the Institute of the Brothers of the Christian Schools. 
	The following objects set out hereafter are exclusively subsidiary and ancillary to the main object set out above and these objects are to be used only for the attainment of that main object and any income generated therefrom is to be applied for the main object only:-
(a)	To advance the Roman Catholic religion in any part of the world.
(b)	To advance the provision of Lasallian education in any part of the world.
(c)	To raise funds and to help raise funds for any charitable purpose or for any of the foregoing objects of the Institute.

The principal object for which the Company is established is the advancement of religion in Ireland, and elsewhere by:
3.1.1	Engaging in educational and related ministries;
3.1.2	Engaging in pastoral and spiritual ministries;
3.1.3	Working for the Christian principles of justice, peace and compassion;
3.1.4	Supporting the religious and other works carried on by the Society of the Holy Child Jesus worldwide and its members;  
3.1.5	Providing for and supporting the accommodation and care of members of the Society of the Holy Child Jesus worldwide; and
3.1.6	Acting as permanent trustees for property and investments of every kind owned by or used in connection with the Society of the Holy Child Jesus in Ireland; 
in accordance with the spirit and mission of the canonical constitution of the Society of the Holy Child Jesus.

The Main Objects for which the Charity is established (the “Main Objects”) are:
(a)	the advancement of religion by supporting people to engage with the spiritual dimension of their being in a contemporary and inclusive world, and to promote the understanding and practice of Christianity in the Catholic tradition and such other works as may be part of the mission of the Roman Catholic religious institute of pontifical right known as the “Sister of the Congregation of the Holy Faith” (hereinafter the “Congregation”);
(b)	contributing to the development of the human person through education in the Catholic tradition within the ethos and charism of the Congregation; 
The Charity achieves these Main Objects inter alia through the apostolic works and contemplative prayer of its Sisters, and therefore the Charity supports Sisters in their religious and material needs in order to promote their charitable works.

(I) to facilitate and support and institute for education and research in Theology in the Catholic tradition at Trinity College Dublin which shall be named 'Loyola Institute';
(ii) the promotion, support and development of the Institute and its operations;
(iii) the advancement of the strategic goals and objectives of the Institute, acknowledging and safeguarding the ethos, identity and mission of the Institute;
(iv) the advancement of religion;
(v) the advancement of education

The mission of the Charity shall be informed primarily by the philosophy and spirituality derived by the Catholic tradition of the Christian Brothers.
The Charity shall:
1. Be ecumenical and respectful of all faith communities;
2. Be supportive of the Mission of Catholic education by assisting processes to articulate the ethos of Catholic education and by proposing models of education to implement it;
3. Provide education programmes to encourage and empower the disadvantaged and the poor;
4. Build a community of learning which is person centred, respectful of individual difference and accessible to people who are disadvantaged;
5. Provide a strong element of teacher education as may be required from time to time;
6. Assist parents to fulfil the responsibilities of their roles as educators;
7. Respect all truth seekers and defend their right to pursue new knowledge wherever it may lead.

The main object for which the Trust is established is advancement of benefit to the community by the promotion of health and community welfare, by providing financial support to the Hospitals in respect of services and activities that are partially funded or are not funded by the State, through paying and applying the annual income of the Trust (and so much of the capital thereof) from time to time as the Trustees shall think fit towards the following services and activities of the Hospitals:

(A)	the administration and management of the governance structure of the Hospitals and the expenses incurred for the time being;
(B)	such support services as the Trustees think fit for the patients of the Hospitals and their families on the patients’ return visits to the Hospitals;
(C)	such support services as the Trustees think fit for the staff and personnel of the Hospitals;
(D)	the provision of pastoral care and mission effectiveness in the Hospitals;
(E)	the provision of health promotion and awareness programmes for the patients and staff of the Hospitals;
(F)	the provision of the Art in the Hospitals Project;
(G)	the provision of environmental welfare within the Hospitals; and
(H)	such other services as the Trustees shall think fit.

The Company is established for the following main charitable objects: 

(i) to provide for the service, cure and/or care of the sick, the aged, the dying, the afflicted and/or the weak in the community in general, the promotion of the well-being of the human person (both physical and mental), research into the promotion of health, the relief, cure and/or prevention of sickness and/or disability and/or into the causes and healing of illness in any of its forms (including, but not limited to, the promotion, establishment and/or provision of medical advice, healthcare facilities, staff, health care treatment and/or any hospital, hospice, nursing home, retirement village and/or community health care service), provided same is, in each case, carried on and conducted in a manner which is in conformity with: (a) the Charter; and (b) Voluntary Status;
(ii) to promote the highest standards of Health Care Services in a manner which is in conformity with the Charter;
(iii) to promote Health Care Services in a manner which is in conformity with the Charter;
(iv) to advise on the governance, management and operation of Health Care Facilities and/or Health Care Services, in each case in a manner which is in conformity with: (a) the Charter; and (b) Voluntary Status;
(v) to own, govern, conduct, control, manage and/or operate Health Care Facilities, in each case in a manner which is in conformity with: (a) the Charter; and (b) Voluntary Status;
(vi) to initiate, promote and facilitate through educational and other programmes the personal and professional development of staff and other persons associated with Health Care Services and/or Health Care Facilities, in each case in a manner which is in conformity with the Charter;
(vii) to carry on any and/or all of the businesses associated with, necessitated by and/or required in connection with (i) the provision of Health Care Services and/or (ii) the governance, management and operation of Health Care Facilities and/or Health Care Services, in each case in a manner which is in conformity with: (a) the Charter; and (b) Voluntary Status; and 
(viii) to ensure and foster the advancement of Health Care Services in accordance with the values and the philosophies as set out in the Charter.

2. Principal Object







The principal object for which the Charity is established is the advancement of religion by:







1) living in community and going on mission, particularly to where life is threatened and fragile, directing their special attention towards the very poor;







2) spending time in prayer by working with the greatest solicitude for all people, respecting their freedom and dignity;







3) endeavouring to create simple and fraternal relationships to serve the cause of peace; and







4) commitment to education, health, pastoral care, sanitary and social activities.

Main Object
The main object for the which the charity is established is to advance the religious, social and charitable works carried on by the Order of the Most Holy Redeemer in Ireland and throughout the world in accordance with the ethos and philosophy of Maria-Celeste Crostarosa.
Subsidiary Objects:
1) To promote the spiritual, moral and intellectual well being of the members;
2) To manage and promote the retreat and spirituality centre having a Roman Catholic Ethos for Christians and to provide opportunities for people to participate in prayer, reflection and religious services;
3) To assist in the relief of poverty and its consequences in areas of natural disasters;
4) To provide comfort and support to the poor and distressed through prayer;
5) To witness and nurture the Christian faith in both the local and wider Community through the provision of worship and prayer space in the chapel of the Charity / Monastery and to facilitate their participation in liturgies;
6) To produce altar breads for sale and as a service to churches and parishes;
7) To promote vocations to the Community and to educate both current and new members of the Community;
8) To encourage religious commitment of people in both Christian and other faith contexts by promoting justice, peace projects and courses and to assist and collaborate with other involved in charitable and humanising work having objects similar or part similar to those of this charity;
9) To provide for the reasonable needs and assistance of members of the order who serve the main objects of the Charity and to provide them with the necessary resources required in the performance of their duties in keeping with the main object of the charity PROVIDED ALWAYS that no part of the capital or income of the Charity may be distributed to any person by way of profit or dividend;
10) To provide assistance to other bodies involved in charitable works having a main object similar to those of the Charity;
11) Generally, to respond to the needs of the people within the mission of the Roman Catholic Church throughout the world PROVIDED ALWAYS that all times the Charity shall carry out the aforesaid purposes and objects in a manner exclusively charitable within the law of the Republic of Ireland.

Article III – Objects

The Objects of the Charity are to advance religion for the public benefit and specifically by:

1.	promoting and teaching the Christian faith in accordance with the worldwide teachings of the New Apostolic Church International which is led by the Chief Apostle as its supreme minister, in such ways as the charity trustees may from time to time think fit, supporting the spiritual care of members, visiting guests and others and fostering spiritual fellowship and the development of the faith;

2.	promoting the interlocking union and instruction of the members of the New Apostolic Church International according to the New Apostolic Creed;

3.	carrying out educational activities, including Sunday School and religious instruction; and 

4.	carrying out other charitable activities associated with the New Apostolic Church International for the benefit of the community;

in the United Kingdom and the Republic of Ireland.

2.1	The principal objects for which the Charity is established are the advancement of religion in accordance with the teachings, mission and canonical constitution of the Order by, in particular but not limited to:

2.1.1	offering religious services and preaching;

2.1.2	engaging in prayer, meditation, spiritual enrichment and study, with an emphasis on the contemplative nature of the Order, and supporting and welcoming people to pray and to join in community prayer;

2.1.3	providing spiritual direction to people in need and offering spiritual retreats;

2.1.4	providing adult religious education; 

2.1.5	carrying out pastoral work and other activities in the local communities to assist people in need (such as activities/social projects in local communities);  

2.1.6	providing care and accommodation to members of the Order who are in need of such care and accommodation;

2.1.7	involvement in parishes or making the Charity’s property available to local communities;

2.1.8	promoting and supporting training and formation of religious vocations and members; and

2.1.9	supporting the Carmelite nuns and liaising with the Secular Order and laity.

The Trustee shall hold the Trust Fund UPON TRUST to pay the capital and any income arising from it for the Charitable Objects in such shares and at such times and with and subject to such trusts powers and provisions as the Trustee may in its discretion think fit and without obligation to make payments (provided that the prior permission of the Revenue Commissioners is sought where it is intended to accumulate funds for a period in excess of two years) and the Charitable Objects of the Trust shall be the advancement of the Catholic Religion in the Diocese of Cloyne and elsewhere with the subsidiary objects:
(a) To provide and support religious education in the Parishes and Schools in the Diocese of Cloyne;
(b) To provide pastoral care;
(c) To administer the sacraments;
(d) To support the alleviation of poverty, economic hardship and disadvantage in the Community of the Diocese of Cloyne; and
(e) To assist and work with any other charitable works beneficial to the Community of the Diocese of Cloyne

The objectives of the Association are: (a) the promotion of sobriety and temperance to be achieved principally through prayer and self denial; 
(b) the advancement of the Catholic religion in such ways and by such means as are though fit by the Trustees or the survivors of them; 
and (c) the relief of poor persons in Ireland.

The main object for which the Company is established (the "Main Object") is the advancement of religion through the dissemination, education and enactment in Ireland of the eternal message of Vedanta according to Sri Ramakrishna, Holy Mother Sarada Devi and Swami Vivekananda, without discrimination of race, culture, gender, language, nationality, or religion. The Company shall operate under the principles, grounds and objectives of the Ramakrishna Math (hereinafter “Ramakrishna Math”) headquartered in Belur Math, Howrah, West Bengal, 711-202, India (the governing authority of the Ramakrishna Math hereinafter referred to as the “Belur Math Board of Trustees") and under the guidance of the Minister-in-Charge appointed by Belur Math. This Main Object includes:
(i)	providing one or more ashrama or monasteries in Dublin, or elsewhere in Ireland, as places of worship and meditation and to serve as an affiliated branch centre of the Ramakrishna Math in Ireland;
(ii)	providing guidance and training to aspirants permitted to join the Monastery by the Minister-in-Charge to aid their development and formation, in seeking to become duly ordained monks of Ramakrishna Math in accordance with its rules;

(iii)	giving talks, lectures and workshops;

(iv)	carrying out inter-faith activities with credible interfaith organisations;

(v)	conducting spiritual retreats and religious celebrations and 

(vi)	providing books, materials, spiritual counselling and guidance.

The principal objects for which the Company is established are the advancement of education and the advancement of religion by:
1.	promoting Catholic education in accordance with Catholic Ethos in Catholic voluntary secondary schools in Ireland and elsewhere, and in particular, working to represent the interests of Catholic voluntary secondary schools in Ireland and elsewhere;
2.	promoting the advancement of education in the Irish Schoolheads’ Association schools in Ireland and elsewhere, and in particular, working to represent the interests of the Irish Schoolheads’ Association schools in Ireland and elsewhere;
3.	carrying out the role of a recognised school management organisation, recognised by the Minister for Education and Skills for the purposes of the Education Act 1998;
4.	without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing, providing an information, advice and support service for all such schools, and, at the discretion of the Board of Directors, any other schools which may wish to avail of such services and advice in areas such as school management, finance, governance, good practice and/or compliance;
5.	providing/hosting of management, compliance and/or advisory services;
6.	promoting educational research and development, endowing educational establishments, carrying on research, sponsoring and commissioning research, and establishing fellowships and scholarships for the carrying on of research and development, at the discretion of the Board of Directors in the broader interests of the education sector; and
7.	working with The Association of Patrons and Trustees of Catholic Schools, the Catholic Education Partnership and, should it be so determined, the Catholic Primary Schools Management Association, to promote co-operation and cohesion between the Company and these bodies.

3	PRINCIPAL OBJECTS
The principal objects for which the Company is established are:
3.1.1	The advancement of education by the promotion of human development and social transformation through education in Ireland and abroad, according to the educational principles proposed by Pedro Poveda – Christian humanist and educationalist, founder of the Teresian Association; 
3.1.2	The advancement of education by the provision of Catholic education and formation including the definition and promotion of the ethos of the Pre-School, Junior and Post-Primary schools in Ireland in the tradition of the Teresian Association, (known as the “Teresian Schools”);
3.1.3	The advancement of education by acting as promoter, trustee, patron and/or manager of the Teresian Schools.

2.1	The principal objects for which the Charity is established are: 

2.1.1	the advancement of education in accordance with the teachings and mission of the Congregation as fully set out in the Book of Life by, in particular but not limited to: 

(A)	the provision of funding to those who are educationally disadvantaged; and
(B)	through working closely with other charities which are active in the field of providing education; 

2.1.2	the advancement of the Roman Catholic religion in accordance with the teachings and mission of the Congregation as fully set out in the Book of Life by, in particular but not limited to:

(A)	the promotion of Christian faith and spirituality through personal and community liturgical prayer, meditation, spiritual enrichment and study;
(B)	supporting and welcoming people to pray and to join in community prayer;
(C)	providing spiritual direction to people in need;
(D)	support of school liturgical celebrations at Our Lady’s School; 
(E)	facilitating the use of its community chapel by Our Lady’s School and by the local community;
(F)	carrying out pastoral work and other activities in the local communities to assist people in need (such as activities/social projects in local communities); 
(G)	the provision of financial support to other religious congregations; and
(H)	providing care and accommodation to members of the Congregation who are in need of such care and accommodation and to support them in their ministries; and 

2.1.3	the relief of poverty or economic hardship in accordance with the teachings and mission of the Congregation as fully set out in the Book of Life.

The main object for which the Charity is established is to advance the religious, social and charitable works carried on by the Congregation of the Sisters of St Joseph of Chambery in Ireland and throughout the world and to provide for the Members throughout their lives within the Congregation.
In furtherance exclusively of the foregoing main object, the Charity shall have the following subsidiary objects:
(a)	To promote the spiritual, moral, and intellectual wellbeing of the Members;
(b)	To assist in the relief of poverty and its consequences in areas of the mission of  the   Congregation and in areas of natural disasters;
(c)	To provide pastoral care in the parishes to which the Members of the Region belong and in collaboration in the wider community in accordance with the core values and ethos of the Congregation of the Sisters of St. Joseph of Chambery;
(d)	To witness and encourage the religious commitment of people to the Roman Catholic Faith in both the local and wider community by promoting prayer and voluntary work designed to develop principles of community; 
(e)	To promote vocations to the Congregation and to educate and train both current and new Members of the Congregation;
(f)	To encourage the religious commitment of people in Christian and in other faith contexts by promoting justice, peace projects and courses and to assist and collaborate with others involved in charitable and humanising work having objects similar to, or parts similar, to those of this Charity;
(g)	To provide services and nursing care for the sick and elderly Members of the Congregation in accordance with the core values and ethos of the Congregation of the Sisters of St Joseph of Chambery;
(h)	To provide for the reasonable needs and assistance of Members of the Congregation of the Sisters of St Joseph of Chambery who serve the main objects of the Charity and to provide them with the necessary resources required in the performance of their duties in keeping with the main object of the Charity PROVIDED ALWAYS that no part of the capital or income of the Charity may be distributed to any person by way of profit or dividend;
(i)	To provide assistance to other bodies involved in charitable works having a main object similar to those of the Charity; and
(j)	Generally to respond to the needs of people within the mission of the Roman Catholic Church throughout the world PROVIDED ALWAYS that at all times the Charity shall carry out the aforesaid purposes and objects in a manner exclusively charitable within the law of the Republic of Ireland.

The principal object for which the Charity is established is the advancement of religion in accordance with the teachings, mission and canonical constitution of the Congregation by, in particular but not limited to:
• participating in the work of God’s salvation of humanity;
• engaging in prayer, meditation, spiritual enrichment and study;
• carrying out pastoral work and other activities in the local communities to assist people in need due to poverty and/or with physical, spiritual and emotional difficulties; and
• providing care and accommodation to members of the Congregation who are in need of such care and accommodation.

The mission began in 1662 in France and continues around the world today in new times and new contexts. The Sisters came to Ireland in 1909
B.I. Art 201....we aim to further the growth of all persons in the image of God so that they can:
- express themselves according to their own identity;
- live in harmony with one another;
- become freer and more responsible with regard to their future;.... (Art 202) speaks of seeking justice...in solidarity with the oppressed...taking action against the root causes of poverty...speaking out against unjust systems...all of which demands courage, credibility and detachment from self.

The Main Objects for which the Charity is established (the “Main Objects”) are:
(a)	the advancement of religion as part of the mission of the Society, by proclaiming the Gospel to the people of our time, taking inspiration from the quiet service of Mary, the mother of Jesus in accordance with the Constitutions of the Society of Mary – Marist Fathers;
(b)	the advancement of education in Ireland and across the world, at primary school level, post-primary school level, third-level, and all other forms of life-long learning. Having regard to the Society’s ethos and charism to show solidarity with the oppressed, the Charity shall support non-formal education especially among the disadvantaged in society; 
(c)	the prevention or relief of poverty and/or economic hardship, specifically by attending to the most neglected in society: the poor and those who suffer injustice. The ethos of the Society is to have concern for the poor in their search for dignity and hope, and to renounce all forms of injustice. In-keeping with this ethos, the Society shall advance the welfare of those who are in need due to reasons of social injustice, sickness or ill-health, youth, marginalised status, economic deprivation, housing instability, and/or imprisonment. The Society is committed to responding to the most urgent needs of God’s people. 
The Charity achieves these Main Objects inter alia through the apostolic and missionary works of its Priests, and therefore the Charity supports the Society’s Priests in their religious and material needs in order to promote their charitable works.

The principal object for which the Charity is established is the advancement of religion in Ireland and elsewhere, in accordance with the spirit and mission of the canonical constitutions of the Society of the Holy Child Jesus. The Charity does this by, in particular:
3.1.	Engaging in educational and related ministries;
3.2.	Engaging in pastoral and spiritual ministries;
3.3.	Working for the Christian principles of justice, peace and compassion;
3.4.	Supporting the religious and other works carried on by the Society of the Holy Child Jesus worldwide and its members; and
3.5.	Providing for and supporting the accommodation and care of members of the Society of the Holy Child Jesus worldwide.

The Main Charitable Objects of the College are the advancement of Catholic religion and education by:
- Developing and supporting initiatives that promote Catholic religion and / or education within the Diocese of Ossory;
- Assisting the Diocese of Ossory in its development of religion and education;
- Encouraging and supporting the ongoing formation of priests and laity of the Diocese of Ossory;
- Support the articulation, development and promotion of a Roman Catholic ethos in the School;
- Promoting co-operation with any other institution, authority or body for the purposes of advancing religion, education and / or formation within the Diocese of Ossory; and
- Completing such other actions as are necessary to fulfil the College’s religious and educational remit in the Diocese of Ossory

in accordance with applicable Canon Law.

The Main Charitable Objects of the Trust are: 
1.	 the advancement of religion by the advancement of the Catholic faith and Christian beliefs in accordance with the statements of beliefs of the Syro-Malabar Catholic Church, in particular by the organisation of the celebration of sacraments and religious services, prayer meetings, missions and similar events;
2.	the advancement of education in accordance with the teaching of the Catholic Church and the training of individuals and groups and enlightening others about the Catholic faith; and
3.	the relief of poverty and suffering and other such charitable purposes for the benefit of the public as are consistent with the doctrines of the Catholic Church;
in each case throughout the Archdiocese of Dublin.

3.1	The principal objects for which the Company is established are the advancement of Catholic religion and the advancement of education in accordance with Catholic Ethos through promoting best operational practice in the exercise of trusteeship and patronage of schools, including:
3.1.1	promoting education in Ireland by enabling patrons and trustees to give contemporary expression to the founding intentions of their respective schools;
3.1.2	promoting Catholic faith formation and dialogue with other faiths in Catholic schools and other education environments that include Catholic students or the involvement of Associate Trustees;
3.1.3	enabling patrons and trustees to exercise their statutory obligations to their schools and their obligations in relation to Catholic Ethos;
3.1.4	providing support to Associate Trustees in schools concerning governance and compliance, policies, property, trustees' obligations, patrons' duties, ethos and financial matters;
3.1.5	enabling co-patrons and patron partners exercise their respective roles in Community and Comprehensive Schools and Designated Community Colleges.
3.1.6	providing guidance and support to Associate Trustees in order that they carry out their legal and educational obligations in a coherent manner as set out in respective legislation, regulations and government department circulars; and
3.1.7	working with the Secretariat of Secondary Schools, the Catholic Education Partnership and, should it be so determined, the Catholic Primary Schools Management Association, to promote co-operation and cohesion between the Company and these bodies.

The main object for which the Charity is established is the advancement of religion by prayer, worship and witness to the transcendence of God, and to advance the religious and charitable works carried on by the Charity in Ireland and throughout the World in accordance with the spirit and ethos of the Discalced Nuns of the Order of the Blessed Virgin Mary of Mount Carmel.
In furtherance exclusively of the foregoing main object, the Charity shall have the following subsidiary objects:
To promote the spiritual, moral, and intellectual wellbeing of the Members;
To assist in the relief of poverty and its consequences in areas of natural disasters; 
To support through contemplative prayer those who spread the Gospel, the good of the Church and the good of the whole human family;
To offer prayer for deceased members, relatives, benefactors and friends of the Order, in accordance with the spirituality and values of the Order;
To provide a Confraternity to promote devotion to the Infant Jesus in honour of the Divine Infant Jesus of Prague;
To witness and nurture the Christian Faith in both the local and wider community through the provision of prayer space in the Chapel of the Monastery and to facilitate their participation in liturgies;
To produce vestments, mortuary habits and to alter linens for sale;
To produce rosary beads, scapulars and other religious articles for sale;
To promote the protection of the environment by a simple lifestyle;
To promote vocations to the Order in Ireland and to train both current and new Members of the Charity in Ireland;
To provide for the reasonable needs and assistance of Members of the Charity who serve the main objects of the Charity and to provide them with the necessary resources required in the performance of their duties in keeping with the main object of the Charity PROVIDED  ALWAYS that no part of the  capital or income of the Charity may be distributed  to any person by way of profit or dividend;
To provide services and nursing care for the sick and elderly Members of the Charity in accordance with the core values of the Order; 
To provide assistance to other bodies involved in charitable works having a main object similar to those of the Charity; and
Generally to respond to the needs of people within the mission of the Christian Church throughout the world PROVIDED ALWAYS that at all times the Charity shall carry out the aforesaid purposes and objects in a manner exclusively charitable within the law of the Republic of Ireland.

3.1	The principal objects for which the Charity is established are the advancement of religion and the prevention or relief of poverty or economic hardship, by
3.1.1	engaging in the living of religious life and joining with others, through collaboration and networking, in responding with compassion to those in need;
3.1.2	supporting those in need, spiritually as well as materially;
3.1.3	supporting religious and other charitable works;
3.1.4	being in ministry with people who are materially poor, in need or suffering;
3.1.5	co-operating with all who make the gospel of justice and healing come alive in today’s world;
3.1.6	seeking to protect the natural resources of our earth and to limit harm to the planet;	
3.1.7	providing care and accommodation to Members;
3.1.8	carrying out pastoral work, social care, care of the elderly and needy, educational activities, the promotion of social justice and other activities which align with the Charity’s mission;  
3.1.9	involvement in parishes and local communities; and
3.1.10	promoting and supporting training and formation of religious vocations; 
in Ireland and abroad.

The main object for which the Charity is established is the advancement of religion by worship, prayer and our way of life, to witness to the transcendence of God, and to advance the religious and charitable works carried on by the Charity in Ireland and throughout the World in accordance with the spirituality and values of the Discalced Nuns of the Order of the Blessed Virgin Mary of Mount Carmel.
In furtherance exclusively of the foregoing main object, the Charity shall have the following subsidiary objects:
To manage and promote discernment retreats to the Monastery having a Roman Catholic ethos for Christians and to provide opportunities for people to participate in prayer, reflection and religious services; 
To promote the spiritual, moral, and intellectual wellbeing of the Members;
To assist in the relief of poverty and its consequences in areas of natural disasters; 
To provide comfort and support to the poor and distressed through prayer, personal contact, listening and correspondence;
To support through contemplative prayer those who spread the Gospel, the good of the Church and the good of the whole human family;
To offer prayer for the deceased members, relatives, friends and benefactors of the Order, in accordance with the spirituality and values of the Order;
To witness and nurture the Christian Faith in both the local and wider community through the provision of prayer space in the Chapel of the Monastery and to facilitate their participation in liturgies;
To produce altar breads and other Church articles for sale and as a service to churches and parishes;
To promote the protection of the environment by a simple lifestyle, and by encouraging awareness on the necessity of protecting the environment;
To promote vocations to the Order and to educate and train both current and new Members of the Charity in Ireland and throughout the world;
To provide for the reasonable needs and assistance of Members of the Charity who serve the main objects of the Charity and to provide them with the necessary resources required in the performance of their duties in keeping with the main object of the Charity PROVIDED  ALWAYS that no part of the  capital or income of the Charity may be distributed  to any person by way of profit or dividend;
To provide services and nursing care for the sick and elderly Members of the Charity in accordance with the core values of the Order; 
To provide assistance to other bodies involved in charitable works having a main object similar to those of the Charity; and
Generally to respond to the needs of people within the mission of the Christian Church throughout the world PROVIDED ALWAYS that at all times the Charity shall carry out the aforesaid purposes and objects in a manner exclusively charitable within the law of the Republic of Ireland.

The main object for which the Company is established is the advancement of the Catholic faith by:
1) supporting the existing Benedictine community of Kylemore Abbey and promoting the establishment of future monastic foundations of the Benedictine order at or near Kylemore Abbey;
2) maintaining Kylemore Abbey, its Gothic Church, its Victorian walled garden and grounds to bear witness to the monastic way of life, to gift Ireland with the Benedictine way (of Prayer, Hospitality, Simplicity, Peace, Conversion and Community) and to preserve for the benefit of the Benedictine Order and the nation its beauty and historic interest;
3) making Kylemore Abbey available to the public as a place of retreat, peace and reflective recreation, and a place at which the Liturgy may be publicly celebrated;
4) promoting an understanding of monastic life among visitors to Kylemore Abbey;
5) the promotion of catechesis and evangelisation among visitors to Kylemore Abbey;
6) the promotion of the work of evangelisation in the area near Kylemore Abbey;
7) taking such educational initiatives and providing such educational bursaries as the Company deems fit;
8) supporting missionary work throughout the world and
9) providing for the needs of members and former members of the Benedictine order of Kylemore Abbey and for the support of employees and former employees of Kylemore Abbey who are in conditions of need, hardship or distress.

The main objects for which the Company is established are:
To sustain and defend the Christian faith; to assist, succor and help the poor, the sick and the afflicted especially those suffering from leprosy; to promote and maintain the principles of Christian chivalry; to work for Christian unity; and to follow the teachings of Christ.;
To carry on within the Ireland or elsewhere the traditional hospitaller and charitable works of the Millitary and Hospitaller Order of Saint Lazarus of Jerusalem to provide, help and encourage work and research, treatment and care in all medical or charitable fields to provide help and support to the sick, injured, poor and those in need, to conduct activities for the furtherance and promotion of health, health care and welfare, to promote the ideals of Christian chivalry and knighthood and to provide material and organisational support to individuals and organisations that carry out the activities aforesaid or any of them;

MAIN OBJECT 
The main object for which the charity is established is to advance the religious, educational, social and charitable works carried on by the Order of Friars Minor in Ireland and elsewhere, in accordance with the ethos, mission and philosophy of St. Francis and to provide for the members of the Order during their lives.
SUBSIDIARY OBJECTS 
In furtherance exclusively of the foregoing main object, the Charity shall have the following subsidiary objects: 
- to advance Roman Catholic education in accordance with the ethos, mission and philosophy of the Order by the support and provision of schools and colleges; 
- to provide outreach services for the benefit of the poor and marginalised, particularly for those who are homeless or affected by drug and alcohol addiction; 
- to provide pastoral care, chaplaincy and support to people in times of illness, personal struggle and difficulty and to assist people through listening, provision of spiritual direction and counselling or other supports as their needs require; 
- to welcome and support the local community and to provide facilities where local groups and charitable organisations can meet to carry out their social and charitable activities; 
- to provide places of spiritual retreat and courses in adult faith formation and religious education; 
- to assist in the relief of poverty and its consequences in the areas of mission of the Order throughout the world; 
- to carry out Roman Catholic Church ministries including, but not limited to, the celebration of masses, baptism, marriage and funeral services, novenas, prayer, confessional and reconciliation services and all other liturgical services as appropriate; 
- to encourage the religious commitment of people in Roman Catholic and in other faith contexts by promoting justice, peace and the integrity of creation projects and to assist and collaborate with others involved in charitable work having objects similar to, or parts similar, to those of this Charity; 
- to promote vocations, ongoing formation and to educate and train both current and new Members of the Order; 
- to provide for the reasonable needs and assistance of Members of the Order throughout their lives who serve the main objects of the Charity and to provide them with the necessary resources required in the performance of their duties in keeping with the main object of the Charity PROVIDED ALWAYS that no part of the capital or income of the Charity may be distributed to any person by way of profit or dividend; 
- to provide assistance to other bodies involved in charitable works having a main or subsidiary object similar to those of the Charity; and 
- to generally respond to the needs of people within the mission of the Roman Catholic Church throughout the world PROVIDED ALWAYS that at all times the Charity shall carry out the aforesaid purposes and objects in a manner exclusively charitable within the law of the Republic of Ireland.

The Charitable Objects of the Diocesan Trust shall be: 
(a) to promote the advancement of the Roman Catholic religion as determined by the Bishop from time to time and without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing by providing for the curia of the Diocese and by operating and applying the funds administered by the Diocese; and
(b) to defray the costs and expenses of the administration of the Diocesan Trust.
In furtherance exclusively of the foregoing main objects, the Diocesan Trust shall have the following subsidiary object:
to promote the advancement of religion by enabling the Bishop to fulfil his Canon Law responsibilities in relation to the advancement or maintenance of the Roman Catholic Religion in any way which he considers appropriate including but not limited to: 
(i) advancement of religion;
(ii) education; 
(iii) provision of priests and other clergy; 
(iv) promotion of Catholic ethos in all walks of life;
(v) relief of poverty; and
(vi) provision of physical infrastructure including but not limited to churches, parish centres, schools, hospitals, hostels for the homeless, places of accommodation, cemeteries and office premises whether directly or through any other charitable body or company and to encourage, promote, set up and support activities, buildings, institutions and services of a pastoral kind and nature in the Diocese including but not limited to the following services: 
a. the development and renewal of any parish or parishes; 
b. marriage counselling and tribunal services;
c. vocations for the priesthood and religious life (for example by providing financial assistance for seminaries, seminarians, deacons, religious institutes and religious); 
d. faith development;
e. communications;
f. services to the travelling people;
g. assistance to emigrants and immigrants;
h. religious education;
i. diocesan committees, commissions and councils; 
j. chaplaincies; 
k. the provision of safeguarding services and relevant training;
l. the provision of training of pastoral workers and volunteers; and
m. supporting the work of the Irish Catholic Bishops Conference (also known as the Irish Episcopal Conference);
in each case in accordance with Canon Law.
And for the Parish Trust:-
Each Parish Fund is held for the Charitable Objects of that Parish alone. No-one other than the Parish Trustee has power to incur liabilities affecting the Parish Fund of the Parish of which he is Parish Priest and only the Parish Trustee may deal with any part of the Parish Fund, subject to him complying in all cases with the requirements of Canon Law. Each Parish Fund is a separate restricted fund. Therefore, the proceeds of a Parish Fund are not available by right to any other Parish Fund or to the Diocese. 
The Charitable Objects of the Parish Trust shall be:
(a) to promote the advancement of the Roman Catholic religion within the jurisdiction of the Parish;
(b) to promote the advancement of the Roman Catholic religion outside the jurisdiction of the Parish provided this is in accordance with applicable Canon Law [and without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing through facilitating the Clogher Diocesan Clerical Fund]; 
(c) to defray the costs and expenses of the running of the Diocese and the administration of the Trust by contributing to the Coordination Fund to pay for the shared administrative activities; and
(d) to defray the costs and expense of constructing, maintaining, improving and repairing churches, presbyteries, schools and buildings of all kinds used or to be used solely or mainly for any of the charitable purposes of the Parish.

The main object for which the charity is established is to advance religion through the educational, social and charitable works carried on by the Congregation of The Priests of the Sacred Heart of Jesus in Ireland and throughout the World in accordance with the ethos, mission and philosophy of Father Leo John Dehon and to provide for the Members of the Congregation during their lives. 
In furtherance exclusively of the foregoing main object, the Charity shall have the following subsidiary objects:
To promote the spiritual, moral, and intellectual wellbeing of the Members of the Congregation;
To advance Roman Catholic education in accordance with the ethos, mission and philosophy of the Congregation; 
To provide places of spiritual retreat, courses in personal development and religious education; 
To welcome and support students from abroad by providing services and accommodation;
To assist in the relief of poverty and its consequences in areas of the mission of the Congregation and in areas of natural disasters; 
To provide comfort and support to the poor, sick and distressed through prayer;
To witness and nurture the Christian Faith in both the local and wider community through the provision of worship and prayer space in the Parish and to facilitate their participation in liturgies;
To promote the protection of the natural environment through education and encouraging public awareness on the importance of the protection of the environment;
To promote vocations and on-going formations to the Congregation and to educate and train both current and new Members of the Congregation; 
To provide services and nursing care for the sick and elderly Members of the Congregation in accordance with the core values and ethos of the Congregation; 
To provide support and assistance in the safeguarding of children and vulnerable adults and ensuring that children and vulnerable adults are well cared for while in the care of any ministry of the Congregation;
To provide for the reasonable needs and assistance of Members of the Congregation who serve, or have served, the main objects of the Charity and to provide them with the necessary resources required in the performance of their duties in keeping with the main object of the Charity PROVIDED ALWAYS that no part of the  capital or income of the Charity may be distributed  to any person by way of profit or dividend;
To provide assistance to other bodies involved in charitable works having a main object similar to those of the Charity; and
Generally to respond to the needs of people within the mission of the Christian Church throughout the world PROVIDED ALWAYS that at all times the Charity shall carry out the aforesaid purposes and objects in a manner exclusively charitable within the law of the Republic of Ireland.

The principal object for which the Society is established (the “Principal Object”) is the advancement of religion, and in particular Catholicism, in Ireland and worldwide, by:
2.1.1.	Fostering vocations to the priesthood and religious life and financially assisting students for the priesthood;
2.1.2.	Promoting the vocation of the laity, fostering a greater understanding and love of the Mass, and helping Members to be Christ’s presence in every aspect of life.
